Harborwatch 1A MVP
An offline-friendly, single-page prototype for the hackathon problem 1A: Naval / Army Geospatial & Multimodal Threat Detection.

Run it
Open index.html in a modern browser. No build step, account, API key, install, or server is required. The interface is designed for laptop presentation and adapts to smaller screens.

Demo flow (about 2 minutes)
Introduce the coastal watch map as an illustrative, simulated operating picture.
Point out the three evidence layers: monitored tracks, alert markers, and the sensor reports in the legend. Toggle COVERAGE to show the sample coverage rings.
Select Track identity mismatch. Explain that the example flags a 3.8 km disagreement between two simulated sources, shows source age and confidence, and suggests checking timestamps and association.
Open Unusual route deviation and explain that a rule-based cue is a prompt for review, not a threat determination.
Use Mark reviewed or Dismiss to demonstrate human control and the live queue count. The HIGH / REVIEW filters narrow the queue.
Close by describing how open AIS, satellite, or benchmark imagery feeds could replace the synthetic inputs in a later iteration, with evaluation against labeled data.
What is implemented
Responsive geospatial-style coastal map with track lines, vessel markers, alert halos, sensor symbols, and optional coverage rings.
Three seeded alert examples with evidence, source age, illustrative confidence, coordinates, and analyst guidance.
Alert filtering, selection from either the queue or map, review/dismiss actions, and live queue counters.
Explicit simulation and data-status labels throughout the interface.
Scope and limitations
This is a hackathon interface prototype. The map geometry and all positions, tracks, measurements, alerts, and confidence values are illustrative and synthetic. Alert examples are hard-coded demonstrations, not model outputs. It does not connect to live AIS, satellite, radar, drone, GIS, or classified feeds. The separate xView scene explorer runs a local vessel model on overhead TIFFs, but its output is not connected to this simulated dashboard. The prototype has no authentication, persistence, or deployment hardening and should not be used for navigation or operational decisions.

The map uses inline SVG and CSS, so core functionality works without network access. Google Fonts are loaded from a CDN when available; the browser falls back to local system fonts otherwise.

Next build steps
An xView dataset audit and local scene explorer are now included. They inspect TIFF imagery, image/label coverage, numeric category IDs, and bounding-box overlays from the supplied GeoJSON.

Run the explorer from this folder with `python dataset_explorer.py`, then open `http://127.0.0.1:8765/`. It uses Pillow to preview TIFFs and NumPy to stretch the 16-bit Sentinel-2 bands for display. Install them with `python -m pip install Pillow numpy` if needed. The server catalogs the small Sentinel-2 metadata first and loads the 355 MB xView labels only when you open the xView explorer. Stop it with Ctrl+C.

Run `python dataset_explorer.py --audit` to write `dataset_audit.json` without starting the web server.

Sentinel-2 imagery and vessel-detection data are cataloged on the explorer's “Sentinel-2 Data” page at `/sentinel2`. By default the page reads only each CSV header and eight initial records. The explicit “Load detections” action streams the chosen Pipe V4 CSV and filters detections to the selected GeoTIFF footprint; first scan can take several seconds and repeated scans are cached in memory. The overlay plots provider detection coordinates; it does not run an independently trained model on the displayed pixels. Always compare acquisition/detection dates before interpreting a spatial match. The supplied `IMAGES/Browser_images` rasters include B02, B03, B04, B08, B11, B12 and a true-color image. GeoTIFF tags identify WGS84 coordinates and allow a geographic footprint to be shown.

The folder is named `sentinal2` in the supplied data and is used as-is. CSV feed samples include inferred vessel detections and optional AIS-match fields. They are not a complete AIS feed. The explorer makes no claim about total row counts, coverage, or data quality until a deliberate full-file ingestion/audit step is added.

Audit finding for the current files: the GeoJSON contains 601,937 labels across 847 image IDs. There are 378 valid training TIFFs and 32 retained validation TIFFs. All 378 training TIFFs have labels; 469 label image IDs have no matching training TIFF in this folder, and none match a validation TIFF. The current first training run used only the 376 scenes that had at least one label in the four selected class groups. macOS `__MACOSX` folders and `._*` AppleDouble sidecars are ignored because they are not actual rasters.

To reduce storage for the India-focused workflow, validation TIFFs outside the broad India coordinate extent (68.1–97.4°E, 6.7–35.5°N) were removed. The 32 scenes intersecting that extent were retained; 249 files were removed, freeing about 7.10 GiB. The retained validation scenes remain unlabeled in the supplied GeoJSON.

The xView data is overhead imagery and does not supply AIS or Sentinel-2 feeds. When those are downloaded, add them as separately attributed sources with their own timestamps, licenses, and coordinate metadata. Do not treat xView detections as maritime threat alerts.

Current detector status and next build steps
A vessel-focused YOLO11n model now runs from the xView scene explorer, using 1024 px overlapping tiles. Its current held-out xView scores are 0.277 mAP@50 and 0.347 recall. Scene-level spot checks still show missed vessels; these metrics do not establish dependable detection. The model is not trained or validated on India-specific imagery or Sentinel-2, and is kept separate from the Pipe V4 detections.
The next accuracy-critical work is to label high-resolution India coastal scenes, include hard negatives, and reserve a separate region/date range for evaluation. Then measure precision, recall, false alerts, geolocation error, latency, and edge-device performance. Add reviewed predictions to the operating-picture workflow and save analyst review history only after the detection results are properly evaluated.

Hackathon-sized object detector
The first real AI feature is scoped to xView object detection rather than a full operational application. See DETECTOR_MVP.md for setup and VESSEL_TRAINING_REPORT.md for the latest vessel-model results. `satellite_detector.py` converts local xView annotations into four broad classes, tiles high-resolution TIFFs, fine-tunes compact YOLO models, and runs sliding-window inference. An initial four-class checkpoint and a vessel-focused checkpoint are saved under `runs/train/`. The xView scene explorer integrates the vessel model for visual review. The separate coastal dashboard remains a synthetic interface demo and is not connected to live or model-generated tracks.

Pipe V4 working demo
The Sentinel-2 page now has a “Load detections” flow for the local `sentinel2_vessel_detections_pipev4_*.csv` feeds. It streams the selected CSV, filters detections to the chosen georeferenced TIFF footprint, plots coordinate markers over the TIFF, applies basic presence/non-vessel/cloud score filters, and shows timestamp, estimated length, confidence and available MMSI match. It does not run a newly trained model on those pixels: these are the published Pipe V4 detections. Image and detection timestamps are shown and mismatch is called out. First scan of a monthly CSV takes several seconds; the filtered result is cached in memory.

Resources needed for meaningful end-to-end evaluation
Complete the missing xView training TIFFs, or provide the exact intended subset manifest.
Provide provider/source, usage license, processing baseline, acquisition time, nodata/cloud-mask, scale/offset, and class/score definitions for the Sentinel-2 layers and detection CSVs.
Provide raw AIS track points with UTC timestamps, MMSI, position, source/provenance, and license. The detection CSV's optional AIS match fields are not a substitute for track histories.
Provide overlapping Sentinel-2 and AIS examples for the same area and time. The current rasters cover the same Mumbai-coast footprint on 2026-04-28 and 2026-05-31; the supplied Pipe V4 monthly detection CSVs include February, March, April, June, July, and August 2026, with no May file. April now supports a same-calendar-date comparison, though exact scene/time alignment still needs verification.
Provide reviewed vessel detections and hard negatives for training/validation, including how to interpret presence, non-vessel, cloud, and matching scores. Include a held-out geographic/time split to avoid leakage.
Confirm target operating region, detection use case, edge hardware, offline requirements, and acceptable false-alert/latency/geolocation thresholds before model selection.
